[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintHexCharLine[0m disagreed on the upper bits of its argument register. gcc compiles the [38;5;10mchar[0m parameter as a narrowing read ([38;5;10mmov %al,-0x4(%rbp); movsbl -0x4(%rbp),%eax[0m), so a caller leaving garbage in the top 24 bits of [38;5;10medi[0m — the [38;5;10mchar[0m analogue of an out-of-range enum value — still sees only the low byte. Rust's [38;5;10mextern "C" fn(charHex: c_char)[0m is tagged [38;5;10msignext i8[0m, so LLVM assumed the caller had already extended it and forwarded the whole register ([38;5;10mmov %edi,%esi[0m). [38;5;10mprintHexCharLine(0x100)[0m printed [38;5;10m100[0m in Rust versus [38;5;10m00[0m in C.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
